  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">An innovative whole-of-society approach to build resilience to health and humanitarian shocks from floods and epidemics in the Maradi and Zinderregions to foster peace and stability in fragile areas</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Niger continues to face acute and complex humanitarian crises driven by armed violence, natural hazards such as floods and droughts, population displacement, acute food insecurity, and epidemics. This project aims to build resilience to health and humanitarian shocks caused by floods and epidemics in the Maradi and Zinder regions by adopting a whole-of-society approach. IOM is strengthening disease surveillance at key Points of Entry along the Niger-Nigeria border to address epidemics, including diphtheria, while meeting urgent needs by providing emergency shelter and non-food items (NFIs) to flood-affected populations, such as internally displaced persons (IDPs), refugees, and host communities. Additionally, the project is enhancing water, sanitation, and hygiene (WASH) facilities, prioritizing vulnerable groups such as women, children, and people with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
